1
00:00:01,000 --> 00:00:04,000
They are silent, deadly, and easy to make.

2
00:00:04,001 --> 00:00:07,000
Launched from missiles, sprayed from aircraft,

3
00:00:07,001 --> 00:00:09,000
squeezed from a bottle.

4
00:00:09,001 --> 00:00:11,000
They kill with terrifying speed.

5
00:00:11,001 --> 00:00:13,000
Called the poor man's nuclear bomb,

6
00:00:13,001 --> 00:00:15,000
they are a growing threat.

7
00:00:15,001 --> 00:00:19,000
Now, chemical and biological weapons on Modern Marvels.

8
00:00:30,000 --> 00:00:44,000
On the morning of March 20, 1995, a new era in international terrorism began.

9
00:00:47,240 --> 00:00:52,960
On that day, 11 small containers of the chemical nerve gas sarin were carried by members of

10
00:00:52,961 --> 00:00:59,520
a fanatical Japanese cult called Aum Shinrikyo onto five different lines of the Tokyo subway.

11
00:01:00,000 --> 00:01:05,700
The polyethylene containers wrapped in newspaper were punctured to release their deadly contents.

12
00:01:06,420 --> 00:01:14,400
As the trains arrived at the stations, carload after carload of sick and terrified passengers collapsed in fits of coughing and vomiting.

13
00:01:15,200 --> 00:01:17,960
5,000 people were injured and 12 died.

14
00:01:19,060 --> 00:01:25,420
Exposure to minute quantities of nerve gas like sarin can cause breathing difficulties, blurred vision, and severe headaches.

15
00:01:26,300 --> 00:01:30,640
Higher doses can cause complete loss of muscular control and death.

16
00:01:31,620 --> 00:01:36,940
Experts believe that were it not for impurities in the homemade nerve gas used by the Ohm Shinrikyo cult,

17
00:01:37,480 --> 00:01:39,440
the attack would have been much more deadly.

18
00:01:40,220 --> 00:01:44,860
Scientists who looked at this closely found that while it killed 12 people,

19
00:01:45,120 --> 00:01:47,600
they would say it was only 12 people and that we were lucky.

20
00:01:48,220 --> 00:01:54,280
That a pure mix conceivably could have been far more toxic and killed hundreds or even thousands of people.

21
00:01:54,940 --> 00:01:58,780
The good news was the group wasn't able to get it right, that it is harder.

22
00:01:59,040 --> 00:02:00,980
It's hard to get the dissemination right.

23
00:02:01,080 --> 00:02:04,000
It's hard to plan and carry out such an attack.

24
00:02:04,280 --> 00:02:06,240
But the bad news was that it was trying.

25
00:02:06,640 --> 00:02:10,460
Earlier, the Aum Shinrikyo cult had launched unsuccessful attacks in Japan

26
00:02:10,461 --> 00:02:14,060
with a biological warfare agent, anthrax bacteria.

27
00:02:14,880 --> 00:02:19,780
They had also traveled to Zaire in an unsuccessful attempt to obtain the deadly Ebola virus.

28
00:02:20,660 --> 00:02:24,020
The cult was planning to follow up the Tokyo nerve gas attack

29
00:02:24,021 --> 00:02:27,640
with similar assaults in New York and Washington.

30
00:02:29,720 --> 00:02:33,640
Aum Shinrikyo became the first group other than a nation during wartime

31
00:02:33,641 --> 00:02:37,260
to use chemical or biological weapons on a major scale.

32
00:02:38,600 --> 00:02:41,900
In simple terms, chemical weapons use poison

33
00:02:41,901 --> 00:02:47,140
and biological weapons use a virus or bacteria to cause sickness and death.

34
00:02:47,141 --> 00:02:53,380
Chemical weapons, or CW, include agents like chlorine, mustard gas, and nerve gas.

35
00:02:53,900 --> 00:02:59,260
Biological weapons, or BW, include agents like anthrax, plague, or smallpox.

36
00:03:00,360 --> 00:03:04,840
These silent instruments of death are often jointly referred to as CBW.

37
00:03:06,140 --> 00:03:09,360
They have most often been used in war when there was a stalemate,

38
00:03:09,740 --> 00:03:14,140
and they've been most effective when the intended victims were taken by surprise,

39
00:03:14,141 --> 00:03:17,560
with no protection and no way to retaliate in kind.

40
00:03:20,000 --> 00:03:22,760
The history of chemical and biological warfare

41
00:03:22,761 --> 00:03:26,840
goes back at least 4,000 years to the wars of ancient India

42
00:03:26,841 --> 00:03:30,860
when soldiers used smoke screens and toxic fumes against their enemies.

43
00:03:32,600 --> 00:03:35,560
A thousand years later, during the Song Dynasty in China,

44
00:03:36,200 --> 00:03:38,280
smoke containing arsenic was used in battle.

45
00:03:39,960 --> 00:03:42,220
In the 6th century B.C.,

46
00:03:42,221 --> 00:03:46,760
The Assyrians poisoned the wells of their enemy with a fungus disease called rye ergot.

47
00:03:48,860 --> 00:03:51,780
The following century during the Peloponnesian War,

48
00:03:52,380 --> 00:03:55,660
the Spartans burned pitch and sulfur to produce poisonous fumes,

49
00:03:56,480 --> 00:03:59,140
which they funneled under the city walls of the Athenian enemy.

50
00:04:00,060 --> 00:04:05,240
There was a long hollow tube where they put a kind of obnoxious mixture

51
00:04:05,241 --> 00:04:09,200
and it actually had billows down at the bottom of them

52
00:04:09,201 --> 00:04:13,420
and you could pump the billows and it would blow this obnoxious mixture

53
00:04:13,421 --> 00:04:18,300
out up at the wall level of the defender.

54
00:04:19,080 --> 00:04:23,440
In 1346 A.D., during the siege of Kaffa in what is now Ukraine,

55
00:04:24,000 --> 00:04:27,560
the attacking Tartar army catapulted the corpses of plague victims

56
00:04:27,561 --> 00:04:31,420
over the city's walls, creating an epidemic among the residents

57
00:04:31,421 --> 00:04:32,860
that forced them to surrender.

58
00:04:34,120 --> 00:04:37,640
It was understood that people who came down with the Black Death

59
00:04:37,641 --> 00:04:42,460
were dangerous to be near because the air somehow might have been contaminated near them.

60
00:04:42,980 --> 00:04:46,600
No one understood that there were bacteria called Yersinia pestis

61
00:04:46,601 --> 00:04:49,780
that were actually causing the illnesses in people.

62
00:04:50,500 --> 00:04:53,980
In America, during the French and Indian War of the mid-1700s,

63
00:04:54,460 --> 00:04:57,920
Sir Geoffrey Amherst, commander of the British forces in North America,

64
00:04:58,480 --> 00:05:02,480
suggested that smallpox be used to reduce Indian tribes hostile to Britain.

65
00:05:02,481 --> 00:05:09,580
In 1763, one of Amherst's aides gave blankets and a handkerchief brought from a smallpox

66
00:05:09,581 --> 00:05:12,780
hospital as gifts to two Indian chiefs.

67
00:05:13,700 --> 00:05:18,340
Smallpox soon raged among the tribes of the region, who had no immunity to the disease.

68
00:05:19,320 --> 00:05:23,533
The idea of using chemicals to attack the enemy was suggested during the U.S.

69
00:05:23,534 --> 00:05:24,234
Civil War,

70
00:05:24,900 --> 00:05:29,920
particularly in stalemate situations, where neither side had a clear advantage in conventional

71
00:05:29,921 --> 00:05:30,621
weapons.

72
00:05:32,480 --> 00:05:42,120
There was actually a proposal for both sides during the stalemate created by the Monitor and the Virginia, the ironclads,

73
00:05:42,600 --> 00:05:48,860
to pour chloroform in the vents of these ships and put the crew to sleep.

74
00:05:49,500 --> 00:05:54,820
Another suggestion was to use a fire department hand pump to spray chloroform on enemy soldiers.

75
00:05:54,821 --> 00:06:00,140
Of course, the one problem with that proposal is that you'd probably get shot

76
00:06:00,141 --> 00:06:03,820
before you ever get close enough to spray the chloroform.

77
00:06:05,060 --> 00:06:07,820
Generals on both sides showed no interest.

78
00:06:08,820 --> 00:06:12,400
At the turn of the century, the world and its army still had no idea

79
00:06:12,401 --> 00:06:14,880
how devastating chemical warfare could be.

80
00:06:15,540 --> 00:06:18,860
But on a battlefield in Belgium, near a town called Ypres,

81
00:06:19,180 --> 00:06:20,540
they were about to find out.

82
00:06:20,541 --> 00:06:21,569
During the U.S.

83
00:06:21,570 --> 00:06:31,860
Civil War, it was common practice by both sides to contaminate local water supplies before vacating an area by throwing in the bodies of dead animals.

84
00:06:39,780 --> 00:06:43,900
In 1915, World War I was a stalemate.

85
00:06:44,800 --> 00:06:49,580
Both sides were finding it impossible to dislodge their opponents from trenches with conventional weapons.

86
00:06:49,581 --> 00:06:55,620
In the late afternoon of April 22, near the Belgian town of Ypres, French, Canadian and

87
00:06:55,621 --> 00:07:01,100
British soldiers were solidly dug in, facing the German line, when the German forces tried

88
00:07:01,101 --> 00:07:02,580
a devastating new tactic.

89
00:07:03,840 --> 00:07:08,120
Waiting until the winds were blowing toward the French and British trenches, they released

90
00:07:08,121 --> 00:07:15,320
168 tons of liquid chlorine from 6,000 cylinders, secretly brought up to the front lines during

91
00:07:15,321 --> 00:07:16,021
the night.

92
00:07:16,022 --> 00:07:20,360
A yellow cloud of deadly chlorine gas drifted across no man's land.

93
00:07:21,660 --> 00:07:27,460
Because it was heavier than air, the gas hugged the ground and settled deep down into Allied trenches,

94
00:07:29,440 --> 00:07:32,820
severely burning the throats and lungs of the unprotected soldiers.

95
00:07:34,480 --> 00:07:41,980
Altogether, it killed 5,000 men, wounded 10,000, and opened up a four-mile breach in the Allied front line.

96
00:07:42,820 --> 00:07:45,900
This stunning attack caught not only the British and the French off-guard,

97
00:07:46,160 --> 00:07:50,280
but also the Germans, who had no idea of the effectiveness of this deadly new weapon

98
00:07:50,281 --> 00:07:55,100
and didn't have enough troops to exploit the breach in the line.

99
00:07:56,540 --> 00:07:59,480
Chlorine gas was subsequently used by both sides in the war,

100
00:07:59,940 --> 00:08:04,300
as was a new and more effective chemical weapon called mustard agent.

101
00:08:04,620 --> 00:08:08,620
A gas mask was no longer sufficient protection against chemical attack

102
00:08:08,621 --> 00:08:12,680
because mustard agent caused severe damage to any exposed skin.

103
00:08:14,260 --> 00:08:16,400
Mustard gas was considered king of the battlefield.

104
00:08:16,900 --> 00:08:19,880
It was very hard to protect against mustard agent

105
00:08:19,881 --> 00:08:23,160
because you, in effect, had to have full body protection

106
00:08:23,161 --> 00:08:27,280
to keep from being contaminated with the mustard agent.

107
00:08:27,500 --> 00:08:28,960
It would create blisters on you.

108
00:08:29,500 --> 00:08:31,720
For soldiers unprotected by gas masks,

109
00:08:32,160 --> 00:08:34,100
mustard agent could also sear the lungs.

110
00:08:34,740 --> 00:08:36,640
It was delivered by artillery shells

111
00:08:36,641 --> 00:08:40,180
that spewed out tiny droplets of oily brown liquid when they exploded.

112
00:08:40,960 --> 00:08:43,540
It had the sharp smell of mustard or horseradish.

113
00:08:44,240 --> 00:08:47,720
To soldiers wearing gas masks, the liquid at first appeared harmless.

114
00:08:48,540 --> 00:08:52,460
But after a few hours, after the mustard agent seeped into boots and clothing,

115
00:08:53,360 --> 00:08:57,920
it could cause huge blisters, painful burns, vomiting, and temporary blindness.

116
00:09:01,480 --> 00:09:05,100
When America joined the Allied forces in April of 1917,

117
00:09:05,101 --> 00:09:09,340
the U.S. Army had no training or equipment to fight a chemical war.

118
00:09:10,320 --> 00:09:12,180
That would change a few months later,

119
00:09:12,680 --> 00:09:15,860
when President Woodrow Wilson approved Gunpowder Neck Maryland

120
00:09:15,861 --> 00:09:18,320
as the site of a new chemical weapons plant.

121
00:09:18,800 --> 00:09:20,460
It would be called the Edgewood Arsenal,

122
00:09:20,960 --> 00:09:24,180
and for decades it would be one of the largest development centers

123
00:09:24,181 --> 00:09:26,000
for chemical weapons in the world.

124
00:09:27,120 --> 00:09:28,560
By the end of World War I,

125
00:09:29,060 --> 00:09:32,260
Edgewood produced 711 tons of mustard agent

126
00:09:32,261 --> 00:09:35,740
and 935 tons of phosgene gas,

127
00:09:36,160 --> 00:09:39,560
which is similar to chlorine, but 10 times more toxic.

128
00:09:40,540 --> 00:09:42,520
By the time the war ended in 1918,

129
00:09:43,520 --> 00:09:46,600
about 125,000 tons of chemical agents

130
00:09:46,601 --> 00:09:48,700
had been used by both sides in the fighting.

131
00:09:51,020 --> 00:09:52,480
After World War I,

132
00:09:52,920 --> 00:09:55,700
a new method of disseminating chemical weapons was developed,

133
00:09:56,320 --> 00:09:58,520
employing an increasingly important war machine,

134
00:09:59,000 --> 00:09:59,700
the airplane.

135
00:09:59,701 --> 00:10:08,000
The next war they envisioned would be waves of airplanes coming in with these large spray

136
00:10:08,001 --> 00:10:13,860
tanks holding chemical agents that would spray on the front lines and on the cities.

137
00:10:13,861 --> 00:10:22,740
And actually, during the Italian Ethiopian campaign, the Italians used chemical agents

138
00:10:22,741 --> 00:10:28,440
from airplanes, dropped bombs, and it was used very effectively against unprepared

139
00:10:28,441 --> 00:10:29,846
Ethiopian troops.

140
00:10:29,847 --> 00:10:36,380
In 1935, Ethiopian leader Haile Selassie emotionally described the nightmare to

141
00:10:36,381 --> 00:10:38,254
the League of Nations.

142
00:10:38,255 --> 00:10:44,300
He said, soldiers, women, children, cattle, rivers, lakes, and pastures

143
00:10:44,301 --> 00:10:47,026
were drenched continually with his deadly reign.

144
00:10:47,027 --> 00:10:49,980
In tens of thousands, the victims of Italian mustard

145
00:10:49,981 --> 00:10:50,683
gas fell.

146
00:10:50,684 --> 00:10:57,320
Half a world away, the Japanese military had extensive programs for both chemical and

147
00:10:57,321 --> 00:10:58,740
biological weapons.

148
00:10:58,741 --> 00:11:03,820
Five years after invading Manchuria in 1931, the Japanese set up the

149
00:11:03,821 --> 00:11:11,040
notorious Unit 731, which under the direction of General Shiro Ishii, performed grotesque

150
00:11:11,041 --> 00:11:15,700
medical experiments on Chinese prisoners of war to further Japanese research.

151
00:11:16,780 --> 00:11:23,000
Unit 731 was ostensibly a water purification unit that was established by General Ishii

152
00:11:23,001 --> 00:11:24,660
and his henchmen.

153
00:11:25,180 --> 00:11:28,440
What they actually did was they used human subjects

154
00:11:28,441 --> 00:11:30,460
while they were still living.

155
00:11:30,800 --> 00:11:33,260
They exposed them to diseases while they were alive.

156
00:11:34,620 --> 00:11:37,380
After Japan's surrender at the end of World War II,

157
00:11:37,900 --> 00:11:40,020
the United States military would grant immunity

158
00:11:40,021 --> 00:11:42,520
from war crimes prosecution to General Ishii

159
00:11:42,521 --> 00:11:45,560
in return for cooperation with American researchers.

160
00:11:48,360 --> 00:11:50,920
Today we look back and say, yes, it was a cover-up

161
00:11:50,921 --> 00:11:52,040
and they should not have done that.

162
00:11:52,041 --> 00:11:58,960
But the Americans wanted to know exactly what the Japanese had learned and to see how it compared to their own data.

163
00:11:59,240 --> 00:12:02,740
As the Japanese were conducting their horrible experiments in Manchuria,

164
00:12:03,540 --> 00:12:08,340
in Germany, a scientist named Gerhard Schrader was developing new insecticides

165
00:12:08,341 --> 00:12:12,800
when he discovered a new type of poison that would revolutionize chemical warfare.

166
00:12:13,500 --> 00:12:16,060
It would become known as nerve gas.

167
00:12:16,500 --> 00:12:21,880
Just one tiny drop could cause complete loss of muscular control and death within minutes.

168
00:12:22,040 --> 00:12:29,440
Usually it has no odor. Sometimes it has a slight Swedish fruit-like odor, but it is

169
00:12:29,441 --> 00:12:32,040
seldom strong enough to give warning of its presence.

170
00:12:32,041 --> 00:12:37,040
Nerve gas blocks the action of an enzyme that controls the transmission of nerve impulses.

171
00:12:37,041 --> 00:12:43,040
This causes wild, uncontrolled nerve signals to muscles.

172
00:12:43,041 --> 00:12:46,040
The first German nerve gas was called tabun.

173
00:12:46,041 --> 00:12:53,200
Two years later, Schrader came up with a new nerve agent called sarin, which was ten times as potent as taboon.

174
00:12:54,720 --> 00:12:58,800
As little as 1 50th of a drop of sarin on human skin could cause death.

175
00:13:01,140 --> 00:13:06,820
Thousands of tons of both taboon and sarin were produced in Nazi Germany, but Hitler never used them.

176
00:13:07,820 --> 00:13:14,160
In fact, neither chemical nor biological weapons were used in combat by the Axis or Allied powers in World War II.

177
00:13:14,161 --> 00:13:21,820
There is a lot of speculation why chemicals or biologicals were not used in World War II.

178
00:13:22,640 --> 00:13:27,900
They range from the notion that Hitler was reluctant to because of his own experience in World War I,

179
00:13:28,040 --> 00:13:31,820
where supposedly he had been exposed to gas and felt that was so horrible

180
00:13:31,821 --> 00:13:35,080
that he wouldn't want to see it used and possibly poison his own soldiers.

181
00:13:35,860 --> 00:13:40,440
There was also an understanding that if one country were going to use it,

182
00:13:40,441 --> 00:13:43,680
it was pretty clear that the opposing country would start using it as well.

183
00:13:44,160 --> 00:13:49,860
Hitler may not have used gas on the battlefield, but he had no compunction about using it in gas chambers,

184
00:13:50,420 --> 00:13:53,920
against millions of Jews and others he considered undesirable.

185
00:13:54,420 --> 00:14:00,340
As opposed to chemical weapons, biological weapons remained a relatively unknown form of warfare.

186
00:14:01,220 --> 00:14:07,100
Although Britain, France, Canada and Japan stepped up their secret research into biological weapons in the 30s,

187
00:14:07,680 --> 00:14:10,540
the U.S. military gave it relatively little attention.

188
00:14:10,541 --> 00:14:14,800
That all changed towards the end of World War II.

189
00:14:17,060 --> 00:14:20,440
German buzz bombs were raining down on England in 1944,

190
00:14:21,060 --> 00:14:26,040
and it was feared the Germans would start loading them with biological agents like anthrax.

191
00:14:26,360 --> 00:14:31,100
This concern helped spur the United States to develop its own biological weapons program.

192
00:14:32,420 --> 00:14:35,120
Production plants were established at Fort Detrick, Maryland,

193
00:14:35,121 --> 00:14:40,960
to produce anthrax, botulinum toxin, brucellosis, and plant diseases.

194
00:14:42,320 --> 00:14:46,260
The most important biological agent was considered to be anthrax,

195
00:14:46,740 --> 00:14:51,000
a bacterial disease that can infect all warm-blooded animals, including humans.

196
00:14:52,360 --> 00:14:56,340
Inhaled anthrax spores begin to grow into bacteria when they reach the lungs.

197
00:14:57,400 --> 00:15:01,260
They cause infection that can lead to severe breathing problems and death.

198
00:15:01,261 --> 00:15:08,460
A wartime British experiment with anthrax spores revealed how resilient and long-lasting they can be.

199
00:15:09,460 --> 00:15:15,280
The United Kingdom, along with the United States, had scientists working on an island called Grunard Island off the coast of Scotland.

200
00:15:15,660 --> 00:15:18,760
And among other agents that were tested were anthrax.

201
00:15:19,400 --> 00:15:24,020
After the war, the authorities set the entire island on fire to decontaminate it.

202
00:15:24,480 --> 00:15:27,340
And the island was ablaze for a couple of days.

203
00:15:27,341 --> 00:15:32,520
months later, scientists went back and started measuring the anthrax count, and they found

204
00:15:32,521 --> 00:15:35,980
that the anthrax spore count was exactly the same as it had been before they left.

205
00:15:40,180 --> 00:15:45,640
Toward the end of World War II, the combination of President Roosevelt's death, the extremely

206
00:15:45,641 --> 00:15:50,520
costly battles of Iwo Jima and Okinawa, and the planned invasion of the Japanese mainland

207
00:15:50,521 --> 00:15:51,276
led the U.S.

208
00:15:51,277 --> 00:15:55,940
Army to consider abandoning their no-first-use policy on chemical weapons.

209
00:15:55,941 --> 00:16:02,320
Many of the chemical warfare officers who knew the effects of chemical agent

210
00:16:02,321 --> 00:16:10,240
said, you know, maybe we can save American lives by going ahead and initiating chemical warfare.

211
00:16:10,660 --> 00:16:12,680
It was actually debated at the highest levels,

212
00:16:13,300 --> 00:16:17,520
but again, the decision was made not to initiate chemical warfare.

213
00:16:18,340 --> 00:16:22,780
In 1945, the atomic bomb effectively ended the discussion.

214
00:16:22,781 --> 00:16:30,220
Chemical weapons caused an estimated 800,000 injuries and deaths in World War I.

215
00:16:31,840 --> 00:16:40,400
After World War II, British scientists came up with a new nerve gas that was considered even better than the taboon and sarin nerve agents developed by the Nazis.

216
00:16:41,340 --> 00:16:42,780
It was called VX.

217
00:16:43,600 --> 00:16:45,493
VX has a longer staying power.

218
00:16:45,494 --> 00:16:51,300
That is, sarin, after a few hours, if it's sprayed into an environment, begins to dissipate.

219
00:16:51,301 --> 00:16:55,860
But VX can stay in place and be toxic for a matter of days and weeks.

220
00:16:56,300 --> 00:17:05,540
As Cold War tensions grew in the 50s, large amounts of VX, sarin, and taboon nerve gas were stockpiled by the United States and the Soviet Union.

221
00:17:06,580 --> 00:17:14,820
In the U.S., sarin was loaded into 1,000-pound cluster bombs, which each contained 76 bomblets loaded with nerve gas.

222
00:17:15,780 --> 00:17:21,220
Biological weapons research was also being stepped up in the 50s on both sides of the Iron Curtain.

223
00:17:21,300 --> 00:17:28,460
In the U.S., a 500-pound biological cluster bomb was designed, which contained 108 biological

224
00:17:28,461 --> 00:17:32,500
bomblets loaded with bacteria or viruses.

225
00:17:32,501 --> 00:17:34,196
During the 1950s, the U.S.

226
00:17:34,197 --> 00:17:38,240
biological warfare program centered at Fort Detrick, Maryland,

227
00:17:38,241 --> 00:17:42,300
was one of the most highly classified areas of U.S. defense research.

228
00:17:42,301 --> 00:17:45,180
Much of it has never been declassified.

229
00:17:45,181 --> 00:17:48,960
Bill Patrick began working as a scientist at Fort Detrick in 1951

230
00:17:48,961 --> 00:17:54,080
and stayed until the biological warfare program was disbanded in 1969.

231
00:17:55,460 --> 00:18:01,960
We had two large mass enemies, a Soviet Union and Red China,

232
00:18:02,540 --> 00:18:06,140
and we suspected that they had a BW program,

233
00:18:07,140 --> 00:18:12,460
and that we, myself included, we considered ourselves patriots.

234
00:18:12,461 --> 00:18:17,120
We put, when we're working with a lethal agent like anthrax or plague,

235
00:18:18,180 --> 00:18:19,920
we're putting our lives on the line.

236
00:18:20,580 --> 00:18:22,880
Suppose you've got a cold and you're coughing a little bit.

237
00:18:23,640 --> 00:18:25,680
The thought immediately comes to mind is,

238
00:18:25,820 --> 00:18:30,600
oh my God, I've got pulmonary anthrax and I'm going to go one way, down.

239
00:18:31,020 --> 00:18:36,160
You are in Fort Detrick, where some 600 military and civilian scientists work together...

240
00:18:36,161 --> 00:18:40,900
From the beginning of the biological program in the early 50s to its demise in 1969,

241
00:18:40,901 --> 00:18:46,940
There were 456 infections among the scientific staff at Fort Detrick, three of them fatal.

242
00:18:48,380 --> 00:18:53,220
We had two anthrax deaths and one death due to hemorrhagic fever.

243
00:18:53,980 --> 00:19:00,800
In these cases, in one case at any rate, the dose that the technician received was just overwhelming.

244
00:19:01,840 --> 00:19:07,840
And even though a therapy initiated, the dose was just too large, and he died.

245
00:19:07,841 --> 00:19:13,580
As a top scientist in the development section at Fort Detrick, Bill Patrick's job was to

246
00:19:13,581 --> 00:19:18,560
supervise the growing of large amounts of deadly viruses and bacteria after they were

247
00:19:18,561 --> 00:19:19,740
approved for weaponization.

248
00:19:21,460 --> 00:19:26,620
Many biological agents were grown by injecting small amounts of a virus into the embryos

249
00:19:26,621 --> 00:19:31,520
of hundreds of chicken eggs, a technique also used to grow various vaccines.

250
00:19:32,220 --> 00:19:34,120
I have a sample here, V.E.

251
00:19:34,240 --> 00:19:36,220
Venswellen equine encephalitis virus.

252
00:19:36,221 --> 00:19:42,420
The powder is pink to red because you grow the organism, you grow the virus in the chicken

253
00:19:42,421 --> 00:19:45,220
egg and then you process the embryo.

254
00:19:45,221 --> 00:19:51,720
And of course the embryo is a dark red due to the infection and the color comes through

255
00:19:51,721 --> 00:19:53,720
the drying process.

256
00:19:53,721 --> 00:20:00,320
Although this sample is no longer infectious, Venezuelan equine encephalitis or VEE is an

257
00:20:00,321 --> 00:20:03,220
example of an incapacitating agent.

258
00:20:03,221 --> 00:20:08,320
Bill Patrick considers agents like VEE more humane than conventional weapons.

259
00:20:08,800 --> 00:20:14,940
I can make a good case for incapacitating agents that kill virtually no one,

260
00:20:15,460 --> 00:20:21,400
yet you can incapacitate the population and then allow your troops to move into the area and take it over.

261
00:20:21,540 --> 00:20:27,400
They can tie up medical support people, antibiotics, what have you, used to treat disease.

262
00:20:28,180 --> 00:20:31,780
On the other hand, it only takes two people to bury somebody.

263
00:20:32,360 --> 00:20:36,360
One biological warfare system the scientists at Fort Dietrich had hopes for

264
00:20:36,361 --> 00:20:38,600
was the mighty mosquito.

265
00:20:39,820 --> 00:20:45,460
In the late 1950s, Fort Dietrich was set up to produce 500,000 mosquitoes a month.

266
00:20:46,040 --> 00:20:51,280
And there were plans for a bigger plant that could produce 130 million mosquitoes a month.

267
00:20:52,000 --> 00:20:54,980
It was believed the tiny attackers could be used offensively

268
00:20:54,981 --> 00:20:56,980
to infect an enemy with yellow fever.

269
00:20:56,981 --> 00:20:57,681
Dr.

270
00:20:57,682 --> 00:21:04,180
John McEwan, M.D.: Musquitoes and other vectors can be very effective in disseminating

271
00:21:04,181 --> 00:21:09,875
disease. The mosquito is not only the ammunition, but it's the delivery system.

272
00:21:09,876 --> 00:21:10,740
We found out

273
00:21:10,741 --> 00:21:17,540
a lot of things about mosquitoes that if you release them and the air, wind is more than

274
00:21:17,541 --> 00:21:21,222
5 or 10 miles an hour, those little mosquitoes are not going to go to the target.

275
00:21:21,223 --> 00:21:21,923
They're

276
00:21:21,924 --> 00:21:23,200
going to be blown back.

277
00:21:23,201 --> 00:21:28,540
In a 1956 test program, uninfected mosquitoes were released from airplanes

278
00:21:28,541 --> 00:21:33,873
and helicopters in Savannah, Georgia, and at Avon Park bombing range in Florida.

279
00:21:33,874 --> 00:21:35,140
Within a day or two

280
00:21:35,141 --> 00:21:42,480
later, people at various locations near cities and towns, army bases, where these had been released,

281
00:21:43,020 --> 00:21:48,980
were definitely suffering far more from mosquito bites and evidence that the mosquitoes had been

282
00:21:48,981 --> 00:21:54,860
released. In the 1950s, the Army was also developing an anti-livestock and anti-crop

283
00:21:54,861 --> 00:21:56,234
biological arsenal.

284
00:21:56,235 --> 00:22:01,440
Rinderpest and hoof-and-mouth disease were studied for use against enemy

285
00:22:01,441 --> 00:22:07,040
livestock, and wheat stem rust became the first anti-crop weapon developed by the Army

286
00:22:07,041 --> 00:22:08,449
Chemical Corps.

287
00:22:08,450 --> 00:22:16,340
A test with a simulated biological agent in stockyards in 1952 revealed dramatically

288
00:22:16,341 --> 00:22:19,980
that the U.S. was highly vulnerable to an anti-livestock attack.

289
00:22:21,320 --> 00:22:26,840
The Army proved in the 50s that our entire beef cattle population

290
00:22:26,841 --> 00:22:29,720
could be depleted within a matter of a few days

291
00:22:29,721 --> 00:22:32,280
by stoppateurs being at several points,

292
00:22:32,520 --> 00:22:34,840
spray those walkways with hoof and mouth,

293
00:22:35,160 --> 00:22:38,420
and that would affect over 90% of our beef population

294
00:22:38,421 --> 00:22:39,880
in a very short period of time.

295
00:22:40,700 --> 00:22:43,740
They were unchallenged, and they did this in South Dakota,

296
00:22:43,860 --> 00:22:45,200
they did it in Des Moines, Iowa,

297
00:22:45,201 --> 00:22:47,980
They did it in all the major stockyards.

298
00:22:48,680 --> 00:22:53,060
Another test in the 50s demonstrated America's vulnerability even more dramatically.

299
00:22:53,680 --> 00:22:58,340
An airplane carrying an aerosol spray tank filled with a biological warfare simulant

300
00:22:58,341 --> 00:23:02,660
flew along the continental divide, releasing its load over the Rocky Mountains.

301
00:23:03,460 --> 00:23:05,960
The results were a shock to the military.

302
00:23:06,540 --> 00:23:09,540
Well, it carried from the Rocky Mountains all the way to the East Coast.

303
00:23:09,660 --> 00:23:13,160
They sensed it in Michigan, in New Jersey, in New York.

304
00:23:13,780 --> 00:23:15,740
That's quite a scary proposition,

305
00:23:16,340 --> 00:23:20,060
but it's been a reluctance to declassify a lot of that information

306
00:23:20,061 --> 00:23:22,380
because it still has relevance to a potential enemy.

307
00:23:22,760 --> 00:23:24,780
There is no real defense against that.

308
00:23:25,800 --> 00:23:27,760
That's the key to it, that there is no defense.

309
00:23:28,140 --> 00:23:31,000
Although development of chemical and biological weapons continued,

310
00:23:31,640 --> 00:23:35,300
no army had used them or had to defend against them for decades.

311
00:23:36,300 --> 00:23:38,600
There were some who believed they were a thing of the past,

312
00:23:39,140 --> 00:23:42,420
but a war in the Middle East would soon change that perception.

313
00:23:43,160 --> 00:23:49,000
In 1963, during the Civil War in Yemen, the Egyptian Air Force reportedly used tear gas

314
00:23:49,001 --> 00:23:52,300
against pro-royalist villages.

315
00:23:52,301 --> 00:23:57,680
Over the next four years, the Egyptians reportedly progressed from tear gas to mustard gas, and

316
00:23:57,681 --> 00:24:02,360
by 1967 were using nerve gas against the royalists.

317
00:24:02,361 --> 00:24:08,220
Nerve gas had never been used before in combat.

318
00:24:08,221 --> 00:24:12,306
and protests about chemical weapons research in the U.S.

319
00:24:12,307 --> 00:24:14,860
were stirred up in 1968, when 3,000

320
00:24:14,861 --> 00:24:17,571
sheep died mysteriously on a ranch near the U.S.

321
00:24:17,572 --> 00:24:19,660
Army's Dugway Proving Ground in Utah,

322
00:24:20,540 --> 00:24:23,390
a major center for chemical weapons testing.

323
00:24:23,391 --> 00:24:26,240
Although the Army refused at first to accept

324
00:24:26,241 --> 00:24:31,700
responsibility, it was finally determined that nerve gas drifted beyond the Dugway area during

325
00:24:31,701 --> 00:24:38,131
a test and killed the sheep. Chemical defoliants developed by the U.S.

326
00:24:38,132 --> 00:24:40,060
Army beginning in the

327
00:24:40,061 --> 00:24:45,786
late 50s were widely used in the Vietnam War to deny the enemy cover.

328
00:24:45,787 --> 00:24:47,280
The most notorious

329
00:24:47,281 --> 00:24:53,400
was Agent Orange, a herbicide that contained a contaminant called dioxin, a highly toxic

330
00:24:53,401 --> 00:24:59,048
substance believed to cause cancer and birth defects. Thousands of U.S.

331
00:24:59,049 --> 00:25:00,400
veterans believed

332
00:25:00,401 --> 00:25:05,340
their exposure to Agent Orange caused serious health problems, which they are still suffering.

333
00:25:08,140 --> 00:25:13,200
Since more deadly offensive agents like nerve gas and anthrax were not used by either side

334
00:25:13,201 --> 00:25:19,262
in Vietnam, some believe these agents were past history, no longer a threat.

335
00:25:19,263 --> 00:25:20,060
That would

336
00:25:20,061 --> 00:25:22,170
turn out to be wishful thinking.

337
00:25:22,171 --> 00:25:25,600
The United States used about 14 million pounds of CS

338
00:25:25,601 --> 00:25:29,940
tear gas and 19 million tons of herbicides in the Vietnam War.

339
00:25:31,380 --> 00:25:37,380
In 1969, President Nixon came to visit Fort Detrick, Maryland, for decades the Center

340
00:25:37,381 --> 00:25:40,524
for Biological Warfare Research in the United States.

341
00:25:40,525 --> 00:25:42,660
He surprised many by announcing that

342
00:25:42,661 --> 00:25:43,361
all U.S.

343
00:25:43,362 --> 00:25:48,140
stockpiles of biological weapons would be destroyed and the development program

344
00:25:48,141 --> 00:25:49,040
would be shut down.

345
00:25:49,600 --> 00:25:55,560
I have decided that the United States of America will renounce the use of any form

346
00:25:55,561 --> 00:26:01,380
of deadly biological weapons that either kill or incapacitate.

347
00:26:02,120 --> 00:26:04,480
A decade later, in 1980,

348
00:26:05,200 --> 00:26:08,260
chemical war was about to make another appearance on the battlefield.

349
00:26:09,720 --> 00:26:12,400
With the Iran-Iraq war in a stalemate

350
00:26:12,401 --> 00:26:15,980
and the Iraqis facing human wave attacks by the Iranians,

351
00:26:16,420 --> 00:26:18,340
Iraq resorted to chemical weapons,

352
00:26:18,840 --> 00:26:21,280
primarily mustard gas and taboo nerve gas.

353
00:26:22,320 --> 00:26:24,840
Iran claimed several thousand of its soldiers

354
00:26:24,841 --> 00:26:27,300
were killed by poison gas attacks during the war.

355
00:26:29,520 --> 00:26:32,260
In the Western Hemisphere in the early 1980s,

356
00:26:32,540 --> 00:26:36,340
the United States was accused of using biological warfare by Cuba

357
00:26:36,341 --> 00:26:40,000
after an outbreak of dengue fever that swept the island nation.

358
00:26:40,980 --> 00:26:45,580
The non-fatal disease causes high fever and severe pain in muscles and joints.

359
00:26:46,860 --> 00:26:49,320
It's spread by the Aedes aegypti mosquito,

360
00:26:49,800 --> 00:26:51,680
the same insect that carries yellow fever.

361
00:26:52,580 --> 00:26:54,920
The Soviet Union sent one of its top generals

362
00:26:54,921 --> 00:26:57,540
to investigate the Cuban allegation in 1982.

363
00:26:59,040 --> 00:27:01,720
When I asked him what was his opinion,

364
00:27:02,560 --> 00:27:07,720
whether or not it was a deliberate outbreak or a natural outbreak,

365
00:27:07,721 --> 00:27:09,500
he said, of course it was a natural outbreak,

366
00:27:09,640 --> 00:27:13,720
because it was a normal Cuban strain of this virus.

367
00:27:14,700 --> 00:27:19,500
But when I asked him, but what did you tell Fidel Castro,

368
00:27:19,501 --> 00:27:22,840
I told him that it was a biological weapon attack.

369
00:27:24,240 --> 00:27:27,860
In 1988, within a month of the end of the Iran-Iraq war,

370
00:27:28,780 --> 00:27:30,820
Iraq was again using chemical weapons,

371
00:27:31,320 --> 00:27:34,580
but this time against its own people, the Kurds,

372
00:27:34,900 --> 00:27:37,080
a minority group who were fighting for autonomy.

373
00:27:37,920 --> 00:27:41,400
The Iraqi gas attacks killed an estimated 5,000 people.

374
00:27:42,220 --> 00:27:44,900
These chilling pictures broadcast around the world

375
00:27:44,901 --> 00:27:48,200
were a shocking reminder of the cold, silent death

376
00:27:48,201 --> 00:27:50,800
that comes within seconds of a nerve gas attack.

377
00:27:54,280 --> 00:27:56,680
In the 1980s and early 90s,

378
00:27:57,240 --> 00:28:01,640
scientist Ken Alabek ran the largest biological weapons program in the world

379
00:28:01,641 --> 00:28:05,080
and developed the most powerful anthrax strain in existence.

380
00:28:06,060 --> 00:28:08,680
Before he defected to the United States in 1992,

381
00:28:09,640 --> 00:28:11,120
Alabek was an army colonel

382
00:28:11,121 --> 00:28:15,260
and the chief scientist in the Soviet Union's Biological Warfare Directorate.

383
00:28:15,261 --> 00:28:19,360
You can imagine it's mid-70s.

384
00:28:20,540 --> 00:28:27,360
It's a time of Cold War, and nobody in the Soviet Union believes the United States.

385
00:28:28,020 --> 00:28:32,940
And we were told that the United States was developing biological weapons.

386
00:28:33,280 --> 00:28:36,700
As a doctor, Ken Alubek was trained to cure people of diseases.

387
00:28:37,460 --> 00:28:41,640
But now as a scientist for the state, he was finding new ways to make them sick.

388
00:29:00,511 --> 00:29:06,040
In 1988, Alibek was deeply affected by the death of a scientist in his program.

389
00:29:06,920 --> 00:29:10,560
Dr. Nikolai Ustinov, a married man with two teenage children,

390
00:29:11,060 --> 00:29:14,880
was working with deadly Marburg virus when he accidentally pricked his finger.

391
00:29:17,320 --> 00:29:20,420
In this case, it was clear from the beginning

392
00:29:20,421 --> 00:29:23,380
that probably it wouldn't be possible to save his life.

393
00:29:23,920 --> 00:29:27,660
As Dr. Ustinov lay in an isolation chamber, slowly dying,

394
00:29:27,661 --> 00:29:31,680
he made notes in a diary documenting the stages of his disease

395
00:29:31,681 --> 00:29:34,440
to help his fellow scientists to understand it.

396
00:29:34,441 --> 00:29:39,040
In the final stages, blood seeped out of the pores of his fingers as he wrote.

397
00:29:46,040 --> 00:29:49,280
When Dr. Ustinov died after three weeks of suffering,

398
00:29:49,760 --> 00:29:52,820
his body was autopsied, and then came a final irony.

399
00:29:52,821 --> 00:29:59,520
They removed some organs, liver, spleen, and some other organs.

400
00:30:00,080 --> 00:30:04,840
This virus was more virulent than original Marburg virus.

401
00:30:05,540 --> 00:30:10,400
And finally, this virus was used to develop a new Marburg biological weapon.

402
00:30:10,700 --> 00:30:17,140
This weapon was one of the most effective weapons ever created in this field.

403
00:30:18,140 --> 00:30:19,068
Although the U.S.

404
00:30:19,069 --> 00:30:23,380
and the Soviet Union had both pledged to end all offensive biological research,

405
00:30:23,960 --> 00:30:27,480
Alubek says the Soviet program continued on a massive scale,

406
00:30:27,900 --> 00:30:29,800
and he was beginning to have doubts about it.

407
00:30:30,400 --> 00:30:34,260
We had half a dozen of production facilities

408
00:30:34,261 --> 00:30:39,700
with the capability to produce thousands of tons of different biological weapons.

409
00:30:39,701 --> 00:30:46,220
You know, this amount would be enough to kill the entire population of Earth,

410
00:30:46,221 --> 00:30:48,280
several times over.

411
00:30:48,920 --> 00:30:52,080
Alabek says he began to suggest at high-level meetings

412
00:30:52,081 --> 00:30:56,340
that it was time to start scaling down the Soviet biological warfare program.

413
00:30:57,000 --> 00:30:59,900
Some military people started calling me a peacemaker.

414
00:31:00,440 --> 00:31:03,080
Peacemaker is a very bad word in Russian

415
00:31:03,081 --> 00:31:06,080
because it's a lot of irony in this world.

416
00:31:06,081 --> 00:31:10,640
In 1992, Ken Alabek defected to the United States with his family.

417
00:31:11,520 --> 00:31:16,160
Bill Patrick, once a leading scientist in the U.S. biological weapons program,

418
00:31:16,220 --> 00:31:18,280
was assigned to interrogate Ken Alibach.

419
00:31:18,880 --> 00:31:20,980
It didn't take me long to learn that,

420
00:31:21,620 --> 00:31:24,140
oh, we thought we had a good program,

421
00:31:24,260 --> 00:31:25,860
but it paled by comparison.

422
00:31:26,540 --> 00:31:29,300
Our production facility at Pine Bluff, Arsenal, Arkansas

423
00:31:29,301 --> 00:31:33,280
could produce about one metric ton of dried anthrax per year.

424
00:31:33,860 --> 00:31:36,520
We thought that was a huge amount of anthrax,

425
00:31:36,940 --> 00:31:39,280
considering, you know, the punch that it has.

426
00:31:40,060 --> 00:31:42,820
When I heard that Dr. Alibach could produce

427
00:31:42,821 --> 00:31:51,816
4,500 metric tons of anthrax. My goodness. There's a difference of 4,500 to one.

428
00:31:51,817 --> 00:31:53,840
But immediately we

429
00:31:53,841 --> 00:31:54,609
hit it off.

430
00:31:54,610 --> 00:32:00,340
We developed an immediate rapport, and today Ken Alabeck is a good friend of mine.

431
00:32:01,140 --> 00:32:06,140
After the debriefing, Bill Patrick and Ken Alabeck were signed to write a joint history of the

432
00:32:06,141 --> 00:32:09,364
Soviet and American biological warfare programs.

433
00:32:09,365 --> 00:32:12,520
As they compared notes, they found a surprising

434
00:32:12,521 --> 00:32:13,600
and alarming pattern.

435
00:32:14,600 --> 00:32:16,900
Every time the U.S. came up with something new,

436
00:32:17,220 --> 00:32:18,940
the Soviets had it soon afterwards.

437
00:32:19,740 --> 00:32:22,420
Halabek and Patrick came to the same startling conclusion.

438
00:32:23,100 --> 00:32:25,600
There must have been a mole inside Fort Dietrich.

439
00:32:26,300 --> 00:32:31,120
He says, Beale, the programs are too similar,

440
00:32:31,340 --> 00:32:34,720
and I had to agree with him because I looked on one side of the ledger,

441
00:32:34,721 --> 00:32:36,520
here's the Soviet program, our side,

442
00:32:37,320 --> 00:32:40,940
and he says we had to have people at Dietrich.

443
00:32:40,941 --> 00:32:46,700
And he meant spies because it was just too precise, the timing was just too good.

444
00:32:46,960 --> 00:32:53,540
When you see so many coincidences, of course, it's clear that something was happening.

445
00:32:54,600 --> 00:32:58,320
Alabek and Patrick mentioned their suspicions in the report they gave to the CIA.

446
00:32:59,120 --> 00:33:00,900
The agency has declined to comment.

447
00:33:02,120 --> 00:33:08,680
When Ken Alabek defected, he also provided more details on an incident that had remained a mystery since the late 70s.

448
00:33:09,640 --> 00:33:12,880
It involved an accident at a Soviet anthrax laboratory

449
00:33:12,881 --> 00:33:15,580
in a remote city then known as Sverdlovsk.

450
00:33:17,060 --> 00:33:19,840
Alubek says he learned that one of the officers in his program

451
00:33:19,841 --> 00:33:23,580
had a few years earlier in Sverdlovsk made a tragic mistake,

452
00:33:24,100 --> 00:33:27,780
which led to hundreds of anthrax infections and many deaths.

453
00:33:28,500 --> 00:33:31,360
One of my officers with the name of Nikolai Chernyshov,

454
00:33:32,000 --> 00:33:36,000
he was a guy who removed a filter from a drying system

455
00:33:36,001 --> 00:33:37,860
and forgot to put a new one.

456
00:33:37,861 --> 00:33:39,780
and he left his shift

457
00:33:39,781 --> 00:33:41,820
and you know when a new shift came

458
00:33:41,821 --> 00:33:43,840
to start drying and milling

459
00:33:43,841 --> 00:33:45,820
procedure, they started

460
00:33:45,821 --> 00:33:48,100
releasing this agent into the air.

461
00:33:49,120 --> 00:33:49,880
At first, the

462
00:33:49,881 --> 00:33:51,940
Soviet Union said the anthrax outbreak

463
00:33:51,941 --> 00:33:54,120
was caused by people eating contaminated meat.

464
00:33:54,940 --> 00:33:55,840
But later, Russian

465
00:33:55,841 --> 00:33:57,780
leader Boris Yeltsin confirmed in his

466
00:33:57,781 --> 00:33:59,800
autobiography that it was caused

467
00:33:59,801 --> 00:34:01,740
by an accidental release of anthrax

468
00:34:01,741 --> 00:34:03,140
from a military laboratory.

469
00:34:04,420 --> 00:34:05,840
The final casualty toll

470
00:34:05,841 --> 00:34:07,820
in the Sverdlovsk incident is not known.

471
00:34:07,860 --> 00:34:11,660
But estimates range as high as 600 dead.

472
00:34:13,260 --> 00:34:18,180
200 pounds of anthrax bacteria sprayed from an airplane over a large city

473
00:34:18,181 --> 00:34:21,240
could kill an estimated 1 to 3 million people.

474
00:34:22,800 --> 00:34:26,640
At Fort Detrick, Maryland, towering above all the other buildings,

475
00:34:27,100 --> 00:34:30,600
is a mysterious vacant structure called Building 470.

476
00:34:31,180 --> 00:34:35,500
The Army says that it just hasn't gotten around to taking the building down

477
00:34:35,501 --> 00:34:38,560
or to fixing the inside so that it can be used,

478
00:34:39,300 --> 00:34:40,840
there are people who suspect

479
00:34:40,841 --> 00:34:45,080
that there still may be some anthrax contamination in there.

480
00:34:45,840 --> 00:34:48,220
They actually decontaminated it three times.

481
00:34:48,940 --> 00:34:53,420
But because of the insidious nature of anthrax,

482
00:34:53,680 --> 00:34:55,300
it becomes a spore,

483
00:34:55,660 --> 00:34:57,720
and it can hide in cracks and crevices,

484
00:34:58,240 --> 00:35:00,980
they would not say that this building is 100% safe.

485
00:35:02,020 --> 00:35:04,120
Today, if they were going to work in the building

486
00:35:04,121 --> 00:35:05,120
with a jackhammer,

487
00:35:05,121 --> 00:35:08,560
the workmen would simply have to have a mask to protect their breathing.

488
00:35:08,980 --> 00:35:12,840
But I can't understand why anyone would want to leave that building up there

489
00:35:12,841 --> 00:35:14,900
if there wasn't something to be concerned about.

490
00:35:20,740 --> 00:35:25,060
Although the U.S. stopped developing biological weapons in 1969,

491
00:35:25,780 --> 00:35:28,420
chemical weapons development continued into the 80s.

492
00:35:29,180 --> 00:35:33,380
In 1987, the U.S. began manufacturing and stockpiling

493
00:35:33,381 --> 00:35:35,200
a new kind of nerve gas weapon.

494
00:35:35,800 --> 00:35:38,340
The so-called binary agents were safer

495
00:35:38,341 --> 00:35:40,980
because they were separated into two different chemicals,

496
00:35:41,240 --> 00:35:42,740
which by themselves were not lethal.

497
00:35:43,580 --> 00:35:46,640
Only when the shell would actually be fired off

498
00:35:46,641 --> 00:35:49,000
would you put the two canisters together.

499
00:35:49,001 --> 00:35:51,280
You fire it, and as it's in flight,

500
00:35:51,440 --> 00:35:53,160
it mixes and creates the nerve agent.

501
00:35:54,100 --> 00:35:55,320
One of the component chemicals

502
00:35:55,321 --> 00:35:57,700
was stored at Pine Bluff Arsenal in Arkansas.

503
00:35:58,320 --> 00:36:01,160
The other was kept at Tuella Army Depot in Utah.

504
00:36:01,161 --> 00:36:05,100
In time of need, they could be brought together and loaded into bombs.

505
00:36:06,660 --> 00:36:07,977
By the late 80s, the U.S.

506
00:36:07,978 --> 00:36:11,560
and the Soviet Union had agreed to reduce their chemical stockpiles.

507
00:36:12,200 --> 00:36:17,520
But the threat of chemical war was about to emerge again, with the Gulf War in 1991.

508
00:36:20,220 --> 00:36:21,883
Not since World War I had U.S.

509
00:36:21,884 --> 00:36:27,760
soldiers been sent into battle against an enemy that had used chemical weapons extensively in prior years,

510
00:36:27,761 --> 00:36:31,960
and had publicly announced the intention to use them against the United States.

511
00:36:33,320 --> 00:36:34,020
U.S.

512
00:36:34,021 --> 00:36:38,480
soldiers received anthrax vaccinations and a nerve gas antidote before leaving for the Gulf.

513
00:36:40,000 --> 00:36:44,320
It was known that Saddam Hussein had both chemical and biological weapons,

514
00:36:44,760 --> 00:36:48,060
but to the surprise of many, he didn't use them in the Gulf War.

515
00:36:48,061 --> 00:36:59,200
I think one of the things that no one understands precisely is why Saddam Hussein didn't use.

516
00:37:00,880 --> 00:37:09,300
Clearly one aspect of that answer has to be that he may very well have felt deterred by the nuclear capability of the United States.

517
00:37:10,100 --> 00:37:13,700
Some of the first targets that were hit were his biological and chemical development centers.

518
00:37:14,780 --> 00:37:17,860
Those were some of the first targets hit within the first couple hours.

519
00:37:18,060 --> 00:37:24,340
In addition, we struck so fast that he really didn't have much time to react.

520
00:37:24,341 --> 00:37:29,940
After the war, Iraq admitted to having nearly 10,000 nerve gas warheads

521
00:37:29,941 --> 00:37:33,040
and 1,000 tons of chemical warfare agents.

522
00:37:34,500 --> 00:37:39,080
In recent years, terrorist attacks using chemical or biological weapons

523
00:37:39,081 --> 00:37:40,580
have become a growing concern.

524
00:37:41,980 --> 00:37:45,900
In 1996, Congress passed a new anti-terrorism bill

525
00:37:45,901 --> 00:37:49,660
to prepare the U.S. for future chemical and biological weapons attacks.

526
00:37:50,660 --> 00:37:54,760
Since then, cities across America have held drills and exercises

527
00:37:54,761 --> 00:37:58,420
and stepped up their safeguards against a possible terrorist attack.

528
00:37:59,420 --> 00:38:03,300
I think the general sense of most of the national security community

529
00:38:03,301 --> 00:38:07,860
is that we run an increasing risk of a chemical attack,

530
00:38:08,000 --> 00:38:12,000
of a biological attack against the United States in the next few years.

531
00:38:12,001 --> 00:38:17,600
It may very well be a matter of not if, but when.

532
00:38:17,960 --> 00:38:22,800
The consequences if a major event does occur can be very catastrophic.

533
00:38:23,420 --> 00:38:26,540
We could have a weapon of mass destruction involving chemical warfare

534
00:38:26,541 --> 00:38:32,940
or involving biological agents that could kill tens or hundreds of thousands of people,

535
00:38:33,240 --> 00:38:39,580
and it would be a huge disaster for us, something that our country has never faced before.

536
00:38:39,581 --> 00:38:47,160
Having specific vaccines available for every possible biological agent in every part of the country is impossible.

537
00:38:47,940 --> 00:38:55,320
Former Soviet bioweaponeer Ken Alubek is working on a general treatment to help victims of biological attacks no matter what the agent.

538
00:38:56,520 --> 00:39:02,940
Alubek is now chief scientist at Hadron Incorporated, a Virginia company doing research work for the Defense Department.

539
00:39:02,941 --> 00:39:10,940
One of our large projects is to develop a preparation to enhance non-specific community response

540
00:39:11,500 --> 00:39:14,980
just to protect against a broad spectrum of biological weapons.

541
00:39:15,180 --> 00:39:18,060
Could be used by troops or by general population.

542
00:39:18,840 --> 00:39:23,040
Along with the real threat of biological terrorism have come phony threats.

543
00:39:23,980 --> 00:39:27,200
Hoaxes are a source of growing frustration and fear.

544
00:39:35,603 --> 00:39:44,173
We have already seen evidence of hundreds of hoaxes whereby people have, either by telephone or in a mailed-in letter to a particular agency, abortion clinics,

545
00:39:44,174 --> 00:39:49,240
anti-abortion groups, churches, medical institutions, FBI offices, have received these hoaxes.

546
00:39:49,241 --> 00:39:57,980
If in your office you open a letter and it says there's been an anthrax attack on you because of the material in the letter,

547
00:39:58,500 --> 00:40:03,700
the first thing that one must do is get in touch with authorities and call 911.

548
00:40:04,440 --> 00:40:06,920
And the next is to stay in place.

549
00:40:07,260 --> 00:40:10,600
Don't go running around and showing the letter and handing it to everybody.

550
00:40:11,600 --> 00:40:17,720
And then as quickly as possible, wash your exposed skin areas, your hands, your face.

551
00:40:17,721 --> 00:40:19,980
washed very thoroughly just with soap and water.

552
00:40:20,520 --> 00:40:23,780
In the unlikely event that such a letter was found to contain anthrax,

553
00:40:24,460 --> 00:40:26,840
treatment with antibiotics would be started immediately.

554
00:40:28,520 --> 00:40:33,000
Chemical and biological weapons have been referred to as the poor man's nuclear bomb.

555
00:40:33,520 --> 00:40:35,100
Relatively low-tech and cheap,

556
00:40:35,101 --> 00:40:40,100
they are much easier for a small third-world country to develop than a nuclear capability.

557
00:40:42,560 --> 00:40:45,140
In addition to the United States and its allies,

558
00:40:45,141 --> 00:40:50,580
the list of countries now believed to have chemical and or biological weapons is a long one.

559
00:40:51,260 --> 00:40:55,060
It includes more than 16 countries, and the list is growing.

560
00:40:56,640 --> 00:41:00,720
Chemical and biological weapons are a method of asymmetrical conflict.

561
00:41:01,180 --> 00:41:02,860
In the jargon of national security,

562
00:41:03,480 --> 00:41:08,000
asymmetrical warfare is something a small country would use if it wanted to attack a large country.

563
00:41:09,260 --> 00:41:14,800
Chemical and biological weapons are at the top of the list of asymmetrical threats to the United States.

564
00:41:15,140 --> 00:41:20,140
One scenario where asymmetry is a factor is North Korea, for example.

565
00:41:20,141 --> 00:41:26,140
One of the concerns that we have had is the possibility that North Korea would use chemical weapons

566
00:41:26,141 --> 00:41:32,140
against our lines of communication, against our air bases, against our infrastructure.

567
00:41:32,141 --> 00:41:37,140
The same states that are pursuing more advanced missile systems,

568
00:41:37,141 --> 00:41:41,140
many of those same states are also interested in biological weapons programs

569
00:41:41,141 --> 00:41:44,140
or chemical weapons programs in some way.

570
00:41:44,141 --> 00:41:49,980
And so we have delivery means added to the weapon itself.

571
00:41:51,080 --> 00:41:56,320
The CIA says North Korea may already be able to reach the United States with a missile.

572
00:41:56,840 --> 00:42:00,940
And soon Iran, and possibly Iraq, will be able to do the same thing.

573
00:42:02,260 --> 00:42:07,520
Many experts worry about what's become of the thousands of researchers, scientists, and technicians

574
00:42:07,521 --> 00:42:13,480
who are working in the vast Soviet chemical and biological weapons programs up until the 1990s.

575
00:42:14,140 --> 00:42:26,980
The concern is that now that the Cold War is over, is that scientists and engineers that were associated with such facilities are in fact underemployed or unemployed.

576
00:42:26,981 --> 00:42:31,480
There's a lot of knowledge in the heads of a lot of people, both at technicians level

577
00:42:31,481 --> 00:42:36,520
all the way up to high level sophisticated scientists who are working in this program.

578
00:42:36,521 --> 00:42:41,960
We have clear knowledge that some of these scientists have been offered money to come

579
00:42:41,961 --> 00:42:45,160
and work for them in Libya, in Syria.

580
00:42:45,161 --> 00:42:50,400
We had thousands of scientists with quite sophisticated knowledge how to research and

581
00:42:50,401 --> 00:42:52,780
develop biological weapons.

582
00:42:52,781 --> 00:42:56,780
Some of them in the United States, some of them in Europe.

583
00:42:56,781 --> 00:43:00,780
I can count maybe 20, 30 or 40 people.

584
00:43:00,781 --> 00:43:04,780
I know where they are located now and what they do.

585
00:43:04,781 --> 00:43:08,780
What is the rest? What they do now, of course, I have no idea.

586
00:43:08,781 --> 00:43:14,780
Both the United States and Russia have pledged to get rid of their chemical weapons stockpiles by 2007.

587
00:43:15,780 --> 00:43:18,780
But destroying chemical weapons is an expensive proposition.

588
00:43:19,540 --> 00:43:21,900
They have to be burned in special incineration plants

589
00:43:21,901 --> 00:43:24,340
that allow no toxic fumes to escape.

590
00:43:25,120 --> 00:43:29,200
By 2001, the United States had destroyed 22% of its stockpile,

591
00:43:29,540 --> 00:43:32,260
while Russia had destroyed none of its chemical weapons.

592
00:43:33,900 --> 00:43:36,840
For centuries, a kind of moral revulsion

593
00:43:36,841 --> 00:43:40,680
has characterized society's attitude to chemical and biological warfare.

594
00:43:41,300 --> 00:43:43,980
But it's been argued that there is no logical reason

595
00:43:43,981 --> 00:43:46,760
to single them out as any worse than conventional warfare,

596
00:43:46,761 --> 00:43:51,160
since dying from a shrapnel wound can be just as bad as dying from nerve gas.

597
00:43:52,280 --> 00:43:56,060
So why do chemical and biological weapons repel us more?

598
00:43:57,320 --> 00:44:02,660
Making somebody purposely sick contravenes a basic sense of human norm,

599
00:44:03,180 --> 00:44:05,820
of wanting to make people well and healthy if we can.

600
00:44:05,821 --> 00:44:10,940
Some people have called this effort to use biological agents or chemical agents

601
00:44:10,941 --> 00:44:12,380
public health in reverse.

602
00:44:12,381 --> 00:44:19,720
this public health in reverse contravenes this essential universal feeling

603
00:44:19,721 --> 00:44:25,040
that people should be striving to make people healthier and better.

604
00:44:25,480 --> 00:44:30,980
I think we can grab onto that and use it as a way to minimize the likelihood

605
00:44:30,981 --> 00:44:33,720
of a biological or chemical use of weapons.

606
00:44:34,300 --> 00:44:38,140
The days of offensive chemical weapons development are a fading memory

607
00:44:38,141 --> 00:44:40,120
at the former Edgewood Arsenal in Maryland.

608
00:44:40,121 --> 00:44:42,160
It now has a friendlier title.

609
00:44:42,380 --> 00:44:46,680
It's called the Edgewood area, and it's used for defensive research only.

610
00:44:47,540 --> 00:44:50,920
But the chilling reality is that in some parts of the world,

611
00:44:51,260 --> 00:44:56,140
chemical and biological weapons are seen as the future rather than the past.

